<h2>Why the word "judgment" does so much work</h2>
<p>The Letters Patent confer an intra-court appeal from the "judgment" of a Single Judge — not from every order. Read too narrowly, the appeal would exist only against decrees, and grave interlocutory injustices would escape correction until the end of trial. Read too broadly, every procedural ruling would spawn an appeal and trials would never finish. <em>Khimji</em> charted the middle course: the word takes its meaning from the <em>effect</em> of the order on the parties\' rights, not from its form or its stage.</p>

<h2>The three categories</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Category</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Appealability</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Final judgment</td><td>Decides all issues before the trial judge — the suit disposed of in whole or part</td><td>Appealable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Preliminary judgment</td><td>Suit dismissed on a preliminary objection without trial; or a vital preliminary objection (jurisdiction, res judicata, limitation, statutory notice) rejected, affecting a vital right</td><td>Appealable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Intermediary / interlocutory judgment</td><td>Orders within Order 43 Rule 1 CPC; and other interlocutory orders carrying the traits and trappings of finality — deciding matters of moment, directly and immediately affecting a valuable right</td><td>Appealable if the finality test is met</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The working test for interlocutory orders</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Effect over form:</strong> the question is what the order does to the suit and the parties, not what it is called or when it is passed.</li>
    <li><strong>Matter of moment:</strong> the order must decide something of substance in the main or an ancillary proceeding — not regulate the day\'s housekeeping.</li>
    <li><strong>Valuable right, directly hit:</strong> the prejudice must be direct and immediate, not remote or contingent on later events.</li>
    <li><strong>Finality in its sphere:</strong> within the ancillary controversy it decides — the injunction, the receiver, the leave to defend — the order must be conclusive.</li>
    <li><strong>Serious injustice if unappealable:</strong> routine discretionary orders causing mere inconvenience fall outside, however irritating.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Khimji's own illustrations</h2>
<p>The judgment supplied a catalogue that counsel still mine. Orders held to be judgments include: rejection of a plaint; refusal of leave to defend a summary suit; an amendment allowing a new cause of action that destroys a limitation defence; decisions on preliminary objections of limitation or statutory notice; rejection of an application for judgment on admission; refusal to add necessary parties in a representative suit; orders granting review; staying or refusing to stay a suit; granting or refusing stay of execution; adverse court-fee determinations; allowing withdrawal of a suit with liberty to refile; and — the facts of the case itself — refusal of a receiver and ad-interim injunction. Against these stand the unappealable mass: adjournments, routine discovery and inspection directions, and the general run of discretionary procedural management.</p>

<h2>The statutory overlays in current practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Section 100A CPC</h4><p>No further appeal lies from the judgment of a Single Judge deciding a first or second appeal from a subordinate court. The LPA survives chiefly on the original side and the writ side.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Commercial Courts Act</h4><p>Section 13 channels appeals in commercial disputes to the Commercial Appellate Division and confines appealable orders to those enumerated — Order 43 CPC categories and Section 37 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 — displacing the general Letters Patent route.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Jurisdictional character</h4><p>Appeals are barred from orders in the exercise of criminal jurisdiction; how far that bar reaches hybrid matters — contempt, writs with a criminal substratum — is under active reconsideration, with the Supreme Court in October 2026 referring the correctness of its 2017 ruling on the point to a larger bench.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Testing maintainability before filing</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Classify the order</strong> — final, preliminary or interlocutory; if interlocutory, run the traits-of-finality test honestly.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Check the statutory field</strong> — commercial suit, arbitration, a special Act with its own appeal code, or Section 100A territory.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Identify the jurisdiction exercised</strong> — original, writ, appellate, revisional or criminal — because the Letters Patent and the statutes treat them differently.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Mind limitation</strong> — in Delhi, LPAs are governed by a thirty-day period under the applicable rules; condonation is discretionary.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Have the fallback ready</strong> — where the LPA is doubtful, the alternative may be a petition under Article 227 or awaiting the final decree; filing the wrong vehicle wastes the limitation on the right one.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article summarises a foundational authority and the general statutory framework for information. Maintainability questions are decided on the precise order and statute involved; this is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
